1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,799
Cent #6129.

2
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:06,160
A penny struck in 1979,

3
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:09,039
made to carry one image: Lincoln.

4
00:00:09,599 --> 00:00:10,320
Look for him.

5
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:13,039
The edge of the profile survives.

6
00:00:13,199 --> 00:00:15,080
The features have worn away.

7
00:00:15,439 --> 00:00:19,120
Compare Cent #25, from 1982,

8
00:00:19,199 --> 00:00:21,120
which kept its mint lustre:

9
00:00:21,199 --> 00:00:23,640
on it, the portrait is still sharp.

10
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,519
The Trait Council calls this Faceless.

11
00:00:26,719 --> 00:00:29,839
85 of the 10,000 Cents carry it.

12
00:00:30,079 --> 00:00:31,600
Where Liberty was struck,

13
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:32,799
something has settled over

14
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:35,640
the letters: Obscure Adhesive.

15
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:38,840
Around it, the copper has warmed to gold.

16
00:00:39,039 --> 00:00:40,719
On the back, it has darkened

17
00:00:40,799 --> 00:00:43,600
to obsidian and bloomed with verdigris.

18
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:45,439
The mark as index.

19
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:47,600
In the words of Steven Reiss,

20
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:50,960
in his article "Materiality and Signification":

21
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,359
"The official icon (Lincoln's head)

22
00:00:53,439 --> 00:00:57,159
fades; what matters are the unplanned indices.

23
00:00:57,359 --> 00:00:58,719
The penny shifts from being

24
00:00:58,799 --> 00:01:00,880
an image to being a time-stamp.

25
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:03,840
Its patina says: I have been around."

26
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:07,680
Only seven Cents are both Faceless and gold.

27
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,359
A coin collector would call this damage.

28
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:12,760
In CENTS it runs the other way.

29
00:01:12,959 --> 00:01:14,480
In the words of David Rees,

30
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:17,359
a collector, in the MAKES CENTS interviews:

31
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:19,280
"The more imperfect the coin,

32
00:01:19,319 --> 00:01:21,120
the more history you can see,

33
00:01:21,159 --> 00:01:22,640
the more value we give it."

34
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,920
Cent #6129: a 1979 penny,

35
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,400
Faceless, with Obscure Adhesive,

36
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:33,200
gold, obsidian and verdigris.
